{"causes": ["The death of King Che Anan in 1342 created a power vacuum that Maha Sawa exploited to claim the Cham throne.", "Ongoing dynastic struggles and succession disputes within the Cham royalty provided opportunities for new claimants like Maha Sawa to rise.", "The relentless southward expansionist policies of the Đại Việt Trần dynasty placed immense external pressure on Champa, necessitating a strong leader to resist."], "effects": ["Maha Sawa's reign led to intensified and prolonged warfare between Champa and Đại Việt, notably resulting in the capture of Vijaya in 1353.", "His sustained resistance, despite significant losses, ensured the continued existence of an independent Champa state.", "Maha Sawa's legacy contributed to the conditions for the later Cham resurgence under Chế Bồng Nga, who would launch devastating counter-offensives against Đại Việt."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "che-anan", "sourceName": "Che Anan", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "maha-sawa", "targetName": "Maha Sawa", "context": "Maha Sawa succeeded his brother-in-law Che Anan as king of Champa in 1342."}, {"sourceSlug": "maha-sawa", "sourceName": "Maha Sawa", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "vijaya-champa", "targetName": "Vijaya, Champa", "context": "Maha Sawa's reign as king of Champa was centered in the capital city of Vijaya, though it was captured by Đại Việt in 1353."}, {"sourceSlug": "tran-dynasty", "sourceName": "Trần Dynasty", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "maha-sawa", "targetName": "Maha Sawa", "context": "The aggressive expansionism of the Đại Việt Trần Dynasty heavily influenced Maha Sawa's reign, forcing him into a defensive posture."}, {"sourceSlug": "maha-sawa", "sourceName": "Maha Sawa", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "champa-dai-viet-wars", "targetName": "Champa–Đại Việt Wars", "context": "Maha Sawa actively participated in the Champa–Đại Việt Wars, particularly resisting the Đại Việt invasion of 1353."}, {"sourceSlug": "maha-sawa", "sourceName": "Maha Sawa", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "che-bong-nga", "targetName": "Chế Bồng Nga", "context": "Maha Sawa's resistance and the ongoing conflict created the historical context for the later rise of Chế Bồng Nga, who continued the war against Đại Việt."}], "places": [{"name": "Vijaya, Champa", "role": "Capital City"}, {"name": "Champa", "role": "Kingdom Ruled"}, {"name": "Đại Việt", "role": "Rival Kingdom"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Maha Sawa", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "maha-sawa"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q6137719", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maha_Sawa"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Maha Sawa's reign was a critical period in the Champa-Đại Việt conflicts, as he maintained Cham independence despite significant territorial losses, directly influencing the subsequent Cham resurgence. His resistance ensured Champa's continued presence as a distinct political entity in Southeast Asia.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-08-04T07:07:18Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30885978740", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Maha Sawa or Trà Hòa bố để was a king of Champa from 1342 to 1360. When his brother-in-law Che Anan died in 1342, Tra Hoa claimed the throne.", "newValue": "Maha Sawa, also known by his Vietnamese name Trà Hòa bố để, reigned as king of Champa from 1342 until his death in 1360, a period marked by intense, protracted conflict with the neighboring Đại Việt (Vietnam) under the Trần dynasty. His ascension to the throne followed the death of his brother-in-law, Che Anan, in 1342, consolidating his claim amidst a volatile political landscape in Southeast Asia. Maha Sawa's rule is significant for its role in sustaining Champa's territorial integrity and cultural distinctiveness against formidable expansionist pressures from the north, even as it endured periods of severe military setback.

During his nearly two-decade reign, Maha Sawa found himself embroiled in relentless warfare. Notably, in 1353, Đại Việt launched a major invasion, capturing the Cham capital of Vijaya. This dramatic event, a significant blow to Cham sovereignty, underscored the existential threat posed by the Trần dynasty's southward expansion. Despite this loss, Maha Sawa demonstrated remarkable resilience, managing to regroup and continue resistance, preventing the complete subjugation of Champa. His efforts were crucial in maintaining the Cham state, even if his reign was primarily one of defensive struggle rather than territorial expansion.

Maha Sawa's reign, though ultimately ending with his death in 1360 amidst ongoing hostilities, laid the groundwork for a subsequent period of Cham resurgence under his successor, Chế Bồng Nga. The persistent resistance mounted by Maha Sawa, despite the capture of Vijaya, showcased the deep-seated cultural and political will of the Cham people to preserve their independence. His era solidified the pattern of intermittent, brutal warfare between Champa and Đại Việt that would define the region for centuries, shaping the geopolitical map of mainland Southeast Asia."}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-04T07:07:18Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30885978740", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1350"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": "1350"}], "newValue": ["The death of King Che Anan in 1342 created a power vacuum that Maha Sawa exploited to claim the Cham throne.", "Ongoing dynastic struggles and succession disputes within the Cham royalty provided opportunities for new claimants like Maha Sawa to rise.", "The relentless southward expansionist policies of the Đại Việt Trần dynasty placed immense external pressure on Champa, necessitating a strong leader to resist."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-04T07:07:18Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30885978740", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1360"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1360"}], "newValue": ["Maha Sawa's reign led to intensified and prolonged warfare between Champa and Đại Việt, notably resulting in the capture of Vijaya in 1353.", "His sustained resistance, despite significant losses, ensured the 